<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Philip IV of France</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1268">1268</a>–<a href="/years/1314">1314</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Also known as Philip the Fair · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/capetian-dynasty">Capetian dynasty</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Philip IV of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, called the Fair, was king from 1285 to 1314. His marriage to Joan of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-navarre">Navarre</a> brought Champagne to the crown, and he seized La <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marche">Marche</a> and forced the Count of Bar to become his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> in 1301. He clashed with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-boniface-viii">Pope Boniface VIII</a>, moved the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papacy">papacy</a> towards <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avignon">Avignon</a>, and destroyed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/knights-templar">Knights Templar</a> to take their wealth.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/philip-iv-of-france.jpg" alt="Philip IV of France" width="320" height="505" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Anonymous · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Philip_IV_of_France_1285-1314.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Philip was born in 1268 at the fortress of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fontainebleau">Fontainebleau</a>, the son of the future Philip III and his first wife, Isabella of Aragon. His grandfather Louis IX died on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusade">crusade</a> in 1270, and his mother died in January 1271 after falling from a horse. In May 1276 his elder brother Louis died, and the eight-year-old Philip became heir apparent. Rumour held that Louis had been poisoned at the instigation of his stepmother, Marie of Brabant, whose own first son was born that month. His schooling was entrusted to Guillaume d'Ercuis, his father's almoner. Through his marriage to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joan-i-of-navarre">Joan I of Navarre</a> he was also King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-navarre">Navarre</a> and Count of Champagne.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Philip was crowned at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reims">Reims</a> on 6 January 1286, aged seventeen. He relied on a professional bureaucracy of legalists, such as Guillaume de Nogaret and Enguerrand de Marigny, and left unpopular policies to them. His reign saw the emergence or consolidation of the King's Council, the Parlement and the Court of Auditors. His opponent Bernard Saisset, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> of Pamiers, said of him that he was neither man nor beast but a statue.</p><p>A feud between English and French sailors in 1293 led Philip to summon Edward I, as Duke of Aquitaine, to his court. Edward agreed to give up <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gascony">Gascony</a> temporarily, but war followed from 1294. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flanders">Flanders</a>, Count Guy of Dampierre renounced his fealty and the Franco-Flemish War began in 1297; Flemish town militias defeated the French at the Battle of the Golden Spurs on 11 July 1302, but Philip prevailed at Mons-en-Pévèle in 1304, and by the treaty of Athis-sur-Orge in 1305 Flanders lost <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lille">Lille</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/douai">Douai</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orchies">Orchies</a>. The wars drove him to debase the coinage, which brought unrest and rioting in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> in December 1306.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Philip expelled the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> on 22 July 1306 and confiscated their property. Deeply in debt to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/knights-templar">Knights Templar</a>, he had hundreds of them arrested in France on Friday 13 October 1307; they were tortured into admitting heresy, and many were burned at the stake. In March 1314 he had the last Grand Master, Jacques de Molay, burned. His taxation of the clergy led to a violent quarrel with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-boniface-viii">Pope Boniface VIII</a>, whose residence at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anagni">Anagni</a> was attacked by French forces in September 1303; this eventually led to the Avignon Papacy of 1309 to 1376.</p><p>In 1314 his daughters-in-law Margaret and Blanche of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundy">Burgundy</a> were accused of adultery in the Tour de Nesle affair. Philip had a stroke while hunting and died at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fontainebleau">Fontainebleau</a> on 29 November 1314; he is buried at Saint-Denis. His three sons reigned in turn as Louis X, Philip V and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-iv">Charles IV</a>, and their deaths without surviving sons began a succession crisis that led to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hundred-years-war">Hundred Years' War</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Philip IV of France” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After <b>Philip IV of France</b> occupied the county in 1300, the townsmen of Bruges rose in the Bruges Matins of May 1302, and on 11 July 1302 an army of Flemish militias, fighting on foot with pikes and the spiked club called the goedendag, destroyed the French cavalry…</blockquote><a href="/empire/county-of-flanders">County of Flanders · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Peter and his successors strengthened ducal administration, and <b>Philip IV</b> made John II a peer of France in 1297.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-brittany">Duchy of Brittany · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Philip IV of France</b> annexed Lyon, formally an imperial city, in 1312, and the popes had settled at Avignon from 1309.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-arles">Kingdom of Arles · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Philip IV the Fair</b> (r. 1285–1314) and his lawyers held that the king was emperor in his own kingdom, defied Pope Boniface VIII and summoned the first Estates-General in 1302.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-france">Kingdom of France · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>After 1234 the crown passed to the counts of Champagne, then in 1274 to Joan I, whose marriage to <b>Philip IV</b> joined Navarre to France until 1328, and later to the houses of Évreux, Foix and Albret.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-navarre">Kingdom of Navarre · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Patriarch Heraclius of Jerusalem called for the Third Crusade from the still incomplete cathedral in 1185, and <b>Philip the Fair</b> opened the first Estates General there in 1302.</blockquote><a href="/work/notre-dame-de-paris">Notre-Dame de Paris · Reception</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fontainebleau">Fontainebleau</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fontainebleau">Fontainebleau</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=48.41,2.7&amp;year=1297" aria-label="Fontainebleau on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4613 471 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Philip IV of France"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5063 741h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5063 741h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=48.41,2.7&amp;year=1297">What was here in 1297? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
